==Russian==
{{wp|ru:}}

===Alternative forms===
* {{alter|ru|дерёвня||dialectal or slang}}<ref>{{R:ru:SRNG|дере́вня|13|8}}</ref>
* {{alter|ru|д.|дер.||abbreviations}}

===Etymology===
{{dercat|ru|ine-bsl-pro|ine-pro|inh=2}}
{{inh+|ru|orv|деревнꙗ|дере́внꙗ}}, from {{inh|ru|sla-pro|*dьrvьňa}}. 

Cognates include {{cog|lv|druva||cornfield, plough-land}} and {{cog|lt|dirvà||field, land}}, also {{cog|lt|dirvónas||fallow field, set-aside land}}. Baltic borrowing (with later semantic change from plough-land to homestead and then to hamlet) is likely considering the lack of cognates in other Slavic languages.

===Pronunciation===
* {{ru-IPA|дере́вня}}
* {{audio|ru|Ru-деревня.ogg}}

===Noun===
{{ru-noun+|e|дере́вня|gen_pl=дереве́нь|adj=дереве́нский|dim=дереве́нька,дереву́шка}}

# [[village]], [[hamlet]]
#: {{syn|ru|посёлок|поселе́ние|село|селе́ние|стани́ца|ху́тор}}
#* {{quote-book|ru|year=1862|author=w:ru:Иван Тургенев<t:{{w|Ivan Turgenev}}>|title=w:ru:Отцы и дети|chapter=[[s:ru:Отцы_и_дети_(Тургенев)/Глава_15|Глава 15]]|location=|publisher=|newversion=English translation from|translator2=Richard Hare|title2=s:en:[[Fathers and Sons (Hare)/Chapter 15|Fathers and Sons]]|location2=|publisher2=|year2=1947|page2=|pageurl2=|text=А́нна Серге́евна Одинцо́ва родила́сь от Серге́я Никола́евича Ло́ктева, изве́стного краса́вца, афери́ста и игрока́, кото́рый, продержа́вшись и прошуме́в лет пятна́дцать в Петербу́рге и в Москве́, ко́нчил тем, что проигра́лся в прах и принуждён был посели́ться в '''дере́вне''', где, впро́чем, ско́ро у́мер, оста́вив кро́шечное состоя́ние двум свои́м дочеря́м, А́нне — двадцати́ и Катери́не — двена́дцати лет.|t=Anna Sergeyevna Odintsova was the daughter of Sergei Nikolayevich Loktev, notorious for his personal beauty, speculations and gambling, who after fifteen years of a stormy and sensational life in Petersburg and Moscow, ended by ruining himself completely at cards and was obliged to retire to the '''country''', where soon afterwards he died, leaving a very small property to his two daughters--Anna, a girl of twenty at that time, and Katya, a child of twelve.}}
# {{lb|ru|collective}} the [[countryside]], the [[rural]] [[population]]
# {{lb|ru|colloquial|both feminine and masculine with animacy}} [[yokel]], [[bumpkin]]
#: {{syn|ru|дереве́нщина}}
#: {{uxi|ru|'''Дере́вня'''! [[это|Э́то]] [[не]] [[пиджа́к]], [[этот|э́то]] [[смо́кинг]].|What a yokel! This isn't a coat, it’s a tuxedo.}}
#: {{ux|ru|[[сидеть|Сиди́т]]? — [[кто|Кто]]? — [[ну|Ну]], [[мужи́к]] […] — [[о|О]] '''дере́вня''', [[а]]? […] [[кто|Кто]] [[ж]] [[его́]] [[сажать|поса́дит]]?! [[он|Он]] [[же]] [[па́мятник]]!|Is he sitting? — Who? — That man. — Bumpkin! Who could throw him in prison? He’s a monument! (Play on the meanings of [[сидеть]], meaning to sit and to be in prison, in the movie, [[w:Gentlemen of Fortune|Gentlemen of Fortune]])}}
# {{lb|ru|collective|dialectal}} a [[tree]]
# {{lb|ru|dialectal}} a place which is [[not]] a [[wild]] [[field]]
# {{lb|ru|dialectal}} a [[rural]] [[community]]
# {{lb|ru|dialectal}} a [[bunch]] of [[fallen]] [[trees]]

====Usage notes====
* Traditionally, {{m|ru|дере́вня}} referred to a smaller village without a church, while {{m|ru|село́}} referred to a larger village with a church.

